From 68929476fcc6be8539007583aa62a89c08fa63d0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: elliotttate <elliotttate@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 18:31:11 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 110/110] Aurora: in-between frames cheaper and batched,
 drawables never waited on unseen

Encoding. gxcore render() binds the whole vertex and index buffers once per
pass (a draw is its base vertex and first index) and sets a bind group only
when it or its offset changed: the render worker's encoding of a busy scene
went from about 4.4 ms a replay to 1.6.

Batching. A draw of the pass's last command's state (pipeline, constants,
pixel constants, textures) whose vertices and indices follow on extends it,
its indices counted from the batch's first vertex. In-between frames draw a
batch as one where its draws' blended blocks (and a particle's blended
vertices) came out the same, and one by one where they differ. Adanmae went
from about 6,400 draws a frame to 3,600, and the sea from 11,800 to 4,600.
DOL_AURORA_GXCORE_BATCH=0 turns it off.

In-between data. The blended blocks (about 41 MB a frame in a busy scene at
120 Hz) and particle vertices are written straight into one of three staging
buffers mapped for the frame and copied on the GPU. queue.WriteBuffer made
and cleared an upload buffer that size every frame: a fifth of the render
worker's time, and the GPU's memory swung by 350 MB. A frame that finds none
back from the GPU after 4 ms shows no in-between frames; one that does not fit
shows none and the next buffers are made bigger. The uniform de-duplication
compares against the staged block instead of a copy kept aside.

Pacing. Overloads (the GPU a game frame behind, the game waiting on the render
worker or the GPU, a late drawable) in 3 of 8 game frames take 60 Hz to none
and back after 3 s calm, with a longer wait after a quick relapse. 120 Hz is
not lowered unless DOL_AURORA_FRAME_INTERP_PACING=1 (a drop to 60 for a stall
already over was felt more than the stall).

The helper thread spins 256 times instead of 4,096 before sleeping, and a
sleeping helper is woken for 32 jobs at once or at the frame's end.

macOS: a window out of sight (another full-screen space, or ours sliding in
or out) asks for no drawable, and its surface is kept: nextDrawable blocked
the game for half a second at every switch, and the Error status of the
texture never asked for dropped a working surface.

From the PC branch: a gather-pipe word straight to the translation worker's
batch, the worker's batch buffer kept, a reused draw plan re-constructed in
place, and the texture layout cache locked for the pipeline compiler.

Diagnostics: DOL_GXCORE_DRAW_DUMP=<game frame> lists every draw of that frame
(TEV and indirect stages, texgens with their matrix rows, textures, registers);
DOL_AURORA_PRESENT_LOG adds encode and present times and the in-between data
sizes. frame_interp_test covers the pacing.
